definition.p:
The linear combination of the vectors \vec{v_1},\vec{v_2},\ldots,\vec{v_n} with scalars c_1,c_2,\ldots,c_n is the vector
c_1\vec{v_1} + c_2\vec{v_2} + \ldots + c_n\vec{v_n}\text{.}
The scalars c_1,c_2,\ldots,c_n are called the weights of the linear combination.

instructions.p:
The diagram below can be used to construct linear combinations whose weights c and d may be varied using the sliders at the top. The vectors \vec{v} and \vec{w} are outlined while the linear combination
c\vec{v} + d\vec{w}
is shaded red.

li.p:
      When c=1 and d=-2, we find

	1\vec{v} -2 \vec{w} =
	\twovec{0}{-3}\text{.}

When d=1 and c is allowed to vary, the linear
combinations lie on the line through \vec{w}
parallel to \vec{v}.

We find the linear system for the weights:
\begin{alignedat}{3} 2c \amp {}+{} \amp 1d \amp {}={} \amp 3 \\ 1c \amp {}+{} \amp 2d \amp {}={} \amp 0 \\ \end{alignedat}
If we construct the corresponding augmented matrix and determine its reduced row echelon matrix, we find the weights c=2 and d=-1.

In the same way, we construct a linear system for the weights whose augmented matrix is
\left[\begin{array}{rr|r} 2 \amp 1 \amp 1.3 \\ 1 \amp 2 \amp -1.7 \\ \end{array}\right] \sim \left[\begin{array}{rr|r} 1 \amp 0 \amp 1.43 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -1.57 \\ \end{array}\right] \text{,}
which shows that there are weights that produce the desired linear combination. The same will happen for any vector \vec{b} that we ask to write as a linear combination of \vec{v} and \vec{w}.

statement.p:
This leads to the equations

  \begin{aligned}
  c\twovec3{-1} + d\twovec43
  \amp =
  \twovec{-11}{-18}    \\ \\
  \twovec{3c}{-c} + \twovec{4d}{3d}
  \amp =
  \twovec{-11}{-18}    \\ \\
  \twovec{3c+4d}{-c+3d}
  \amp =
  \twovec{-11}{-18}    \\ \\
  \end{aligned}

Equating the components of the vectors on each side of the equation, we arrive at the linear system
\begin{alignedat}{3} 3a \amp {}+{} \amp 4b \amp {}={} \amp -11 \\ -a \amp {}+{} \amp 3b \amp {}={} \amp -18 \ \end{alignedat}
This means that \vec{b} is a linear combination of \vec{v} and \vec{w} if this linear system is consistent.

The vector \vec{b} is a linear combination of the vectors
\vec{v_1},\vec{v_2},\ldots,\vec{v_n} if and only if the linear
system corresponding to the augmented matrix

  \left[
  \begin{array}{rrrr|r}
  \vec{v_1} \amp \vec{v_2} \amp \ldots \amp \vec{v_n} \amp 
  \vec{b}
  \end{array}
  \right]

is consistent.  A solution to this linear system gives
weights c_1,c_2,\ldots,c_n such that

  c_1\vec{v_1} + c_2\vec{v_2} + \ldots + c_n\vec{v_n} = \vec{b}
.

li.p:
Consider the following linear system.
\begin{alignedat}{4} 3x_1 \amp {}+{} \amp 2x_2 \amp {}-{} x_3 \amp {}={} \amp 4 \\ x_1 \amp \amp \amp {}+{} 2x_3 \amp {}={} \amp 0 \\ -x_1 \amp {}-{} \amp x_2 \amp {}+{} 3x_3 \amp {}={} \amp 1 \\ \end{alignedat}
Identify vectors \vec{v_1}, \vec{v_2}, \vec{v_3}, and \vec{b} such that the question "Is this linear system consistent?" is equivalent to the question "Can \vec{b} be expressed as a linear combination of \vec{v_1}, \vec{v_2}, and \vec{v_3}?"

We find the linear system
\begin{alignedat}{4} 4c_1 \amp {}+{} \amp c_2 \amp {}-{} \amp 2c_3 \amp {}={} \amp 0 \\ \amp \amp -3c_2 \amp {}+{} \amp c_3 \amp {}={} \amp 1 \\ 2c_1 \amp {}+{} \amp 3c_2 \amp {}+{} \amp c_3 \amp {}={} \amp 2 \\ c_1 \amp {}+{} \amp c_2 \amp \amp \amp {}={} \amp -2 \\ \end{alignedat}
with corresponding augmented matrix
\left[\begin{array}{rrr|r} 4 \amp 1 \amp -2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -3 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\ 2 \amp 3 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -2 \end{array}\right] \sim \left[\begin{array}{rrr|r} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{array}\right]\text{.}
This shows that the linear system is inconsistent so there are no such weights c_1, c_2, and c_3. This means that \vec{b} is not a linear combination of \vec{v_1}, \vec{v_2}, and \vec{v_3}.

This is the same as asking if the linear system corresponding to the following augmented matrix is consistent:
\left[\begin{array}{rrr|r} 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\ -2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 3 \\ 1 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp -1 \end{array}\right] \sim \left[\begin{array}{rrr|r} 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -2 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \end{array}\right]\text{.}
From the reduced row echelon form, we see that the system is consistent, which means that \vec{b} can be expressed as a linear combination of \vec{v_1}, \vec{v_2}, and \vec{v_3}. Moreover, there are infinitely many ways in which we can do so.

We find the augmented matrix
\left[\begin{array}{rrr|r} 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ -2 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp 8 \\ 1 \amp -1 \amp -2 \amp -4 \end{array}\right] \sim \left[\begin{array}{rrr|r} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -4 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \end{array}\right]\text{.}
This shows that \vec{b} can be expressed as a linear combination of \vec{v_1}, \vec{v_2}, and \vec{v_3} in exactly one way.

statement.p:
These vectors appear to lie on the same line, a fact that becomes apparent once we notice that \vec{w} = -2\vec{v}. Intuitively, we think of the linear combination
c\vec{v} + d\vec{w}
as the result of walking c times in the \vec{v} direction and d times in the \vec{w} direction. With these vectors, we are always walking along the same line so it would seem that any linear combination of these vectors should lie on the same line. In addition, a vector that is not on the line, say \vec{b} = \twovec30, should be not be expressible as a linear combination of \vec{v} and \vec{w}.

Notice that the reduced row echelon form of the coefficient
matrix

  \begin{bmatrix}
  \vec{v} \amp \vec{w} \\
  \end{bmatrix}
  =
  \begin{bmatrix}
  -1 \amp 2 \\
  1  \amp -2 \\
  \end{bmatrix}
  \sim
  \begin{bmatrix}
  1 \amp -2 \\
  0 \amp 0 \\
  \end{bmatrix}

tells us to expect this. Since there is not a pivot position
in the
second row of the coefficient matrix \begin{bmatrix}\vec{v}
\amp \vec{w} \end{bmatrix}, it is possible for a
pivot position to appear in the rightmost column of the
augmented matrix

  \left[
  \begin{array}{rr|r} \vec{v} \amp \vec{w} \amp \vec{b} \end{array}
  \right]

for some choice of \vec{b}.
